{
  "title": "BM25 vs dense embeddings: hybrid search reality",
  "content": "It's a one-line fix once you know where to look, and expensive until then. The first time mutual tls fails in production, it rarely fails in the way any postmortem template expects. Here's the root cause, not just the symptom.\n\nBM25 and dense embeddings fail in ways that are almost perfectly complementary. BM25, the ranking function behind Lucene and Elasticsearch's default `match` query, is a lexical scorer: it counts term occurrences, weights rare terms by inverse document frequency, and normalizes for document length. It will find a product SKU, a statute number, or a person's surname with near-perfect recall, and it will return nothing useful when the user types \"how do I stop my laptop from overheating\" and the document says \"thermal throttling.\" Dense retrieval, which encodes queries and documents into vectors and ranks by cosine similarity or dot product, does the reverse: it handles paraphrase and synonymy gracefully, and it routinely misses exact identifiers, rare tokens, and out-of-vocabulary strings because the tokenizer fragments them into meaningless subwords.\n\nThe standard fix is hybrid retrieval: run both, then fuse the ranked lists. The non-obvious part — the part the official docs of most vector databases gloss over — is that fusion is not a free lunch. The two scores live on incomparable scales, the fusion method you pick silently determines which retriever wins ties, and the evaluation you run to \"prove\" hybrid is better is usually contaminated by the same lexical overlap that makes BM25 look good. This post is about the mechanics of that fusion, where each retriever breaks, and how to decide whether you need both.\n\n## Why a single retriever keeps losing\n\nThe failure mode that pushes teams toward hybrid is easy to state and hard to fix with one model. Consider a corpus of support tickets. A user searches for \"ERR_CONNECTION_RESET.\" BM25 tokenizes on the underscore and matches the exact string in the ticket body. A dense model trained on general text may map that query near \"network error,\" \"connection refused,\" and \"timeout,\" and return a semantically adjacent but operationally wrong ticket. Conversely, a user searches for \"can't log in after password change\" and the relevant doc is titled \"Credential cache invalidation on rotation.\" BM25 sees almost no overlapping terms; the dense model sees the paraphrase.\n\nNeither retriever is wrong. They are measuring different things: lexical overlap versus learned semantic proximity. The reason hybrid works is that the errors are weakly correlated. If BM25 misses a document because of vocabulary mismatch, the dense retriever may still surface it, and vice versa. Fusion exploits that independence — but only if the fusion step is done correctly.\n\n## BM25 on its own terms: what the parameters actually do\n\nBM25's scoring formula has two tunable parameters, `k1` and `b`, and most teams never touch them. That is usually fine, but understanding them explains BM25's failure modes. `k1` (commonly 1.2–2.0) controls term-frequency saturation: how quickly repeated occurrences of a term stop adding to the score. `b` (commonly 0.75) controls length normalization: how much a long document is penalized for containing the query term. Setting `b` to 0 disables length normalization; setting it to 1 applies full normalization.\n\nThe practical consequence: BM25 is brittle to tokenization. If your analyzer lowercases, stems, and strips punctuation, \"C++\" and \"C#\" may collapse into the same token, and \"v2.1\" may become \"v2\" and \"1.\" The fix is not a better ranker; it is a better analyzer. In Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, that means defining a custom analyzer with appropriate token filters and, for identifier-heavy corpora, a `keyword` sub-field for exact matching. A hybrid system that feeds badly tokenized text to BM25 gets a weak lexical signal and then blames BM25.\n\nBM25 also has no notion of query intent beyond term presence. It cannot know that \"apple\" in a recipe corpus means fruit and in a hardware corpus means the company. Dense retrieval handles that disambiguation through context; BM25 cannot without query expansion or field boosts.\n\n## Dense retrieval on its own terms: the embedding is the product\n\nDense retrieval quality is dominated by the embedding model, not the vector index. Two models with the same dimensionality can differ substantially on retrieval benchmarks, and the difference is usually domain fit: a model trained on web text will underperform on legal or biomedical text unless fine-tuned or replaced. The vector database (FAISS, pgvector, Qdrant, Weaviate, Milvus — all real, all with different tradeoffs) mostly determines latency and operational cost, not ranking quality.\n\nThe failure modes specific to dense retrieval:\n\n- **Exact-match blindness.** Rare tokens, product codes, and proper nouns get fragmented by the tokenizer. A query for \"ISO-8601\" may embed near \"date format\" and retrieve generic date-formatting docs instead of the standard itself.\n- **Negative and numeric queries.** \"Not available in Canada\" and \"available in Canada\" embed close together. Dense models are poor at negation and at precise numeric constraints.\n- **Out-of-domain drift.** A model trained on one distribution will place unfamiliar queries in a region of the vector space that does not correspond to the corpus.\n- **Truncation.** Most embedding models have a maximum input length (commonly 512 tokens for BERT-family models). Long documents get truncated, and whatever falls off the end is invisible to retrieval.\n\nThe last point is underappreciated. If you embed whole documents without chunking, you are silently discarding content. Chunking strategy — fixed-size with overlap, sentence-aware, or semantic — changes recall more than the choice of vector index.\n\n## Fusing the two lists: RRF, weighted score, and the scale problem\n\nThe fusion method is where hybrid systems quietly succeed or fail. There are three common approaches, and they are not equivalent.\n\n**Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)** ignores raw scores entirely and combines ranks:\n\n```\nscore(d) = sum over retrievers r of 1 / (k + rank_r(d))\n```\n\nwhere `k` is a smoothing constant, commonly 60. RRF is popular because it is scale-free: it does not care that BM25 scores range from 0 to 30 while cosine similarities range from -1 to 1. It is robust and hard to tune wrong. Its weakness is that it discards score magnitude — a document that BM25 ranks first by a huge margin is treated the same as one that barely edges out second place.\n\n**Weighted score fusion** normalizes each retriever's scores (min-max, z-score, or softmax) and combines them with a weight `alpha`:\n\n```\nscore(d) = alpha * norm(bm25(d)) + (1 - alpha) * norm(dense(d))\n```\n\nThis preserves magnitude but introduces two problems. First, normalization is query-dependent: min-max normalization on a query where BM25 returns one strong match and nine weak ones produces a very different distribution than a query with ten equally strong matches. Second, `alpha` is a hyperparameter that must be tuned on a labeled set, and it is corpus-specific. A value that works for product search will not transfer to legal search.\n\n**Learned fusion** (a cross-encoder reranker or a trained linear combination) is the highest-quality option and the most expensive. A cross-encoder takes the query and each candidate document jointly and scores relevance directly, which captures interactions that neither retriever can. The cost is latency: cross-encoders must run per candidate, so they are typically applied to a top-k list from the fused retrievers, not to the full corpus.\n\n| Axis | BM25 | Dense | Hybrid (RRF) | Hybrid (weighted) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Exact identifier recall | High | Low | High | High |\n| Paraphrase recall | Low | High | High | High |\n| Score comparability | N/A | N/A | Rank-based, no normalization | Requires normalization |\n| Tuning burden | Analyzer + k1/b | Embedding model + chunking | Low (k) | High (alpha, normalization) |\n| Latency | Low | Moderate | Sum of both | Sum of both |\n| Interpretability | High (term matches) | Low | Moderate | Moderate |\n| Failure mode | Vocabulary mismatch | Exact-match blindness | Dilutes a strong signal | Alpha mis-set for query type |\n\n## Where the head-to-head actually turns\n\nThe interesting comparison is not \"which retriever is better\" — it is \"which fusion strategy matches this corpus.\" Three axes decide it.\n\n**Query type distribution.** If most queries contain identifiers, codes, or proper nouns, BM25 carries more signal and weighted fusion with a higher `alpha` (favoring BM25) is appropriate. If queries are natural-language questions, dense carries more and `alpha` should favor it. RRF sidesteps the choice but underweights the dominant retriever on queries where one clearly wins.\n\n**Labeled data availability.** Weighted fusion and learned reranking require relevance judgments. Without them, `alpha` is a guess, and a guessed `alpha` can underperform either retriever alone on a subset of queries. RRF needs no labels and is the correct default when you cannot measure.\n\n**Latency budget.** Running two retrievers doubles the retrieval cost before fusion. For a p99 budget under 100 ms, that matters. One mitigation is to run BM25 first, and only run dense retrieval when BM25's top score falls below a threshold — a cascade rather than a parallel hybrid. This is cheaper but risks missing paraphrase matches that BM25 would never surface, so the threshold must be set conservatively.\n\nA subtle evaluation trap: if your test queries are drawn from the same vocabulary as your documents, BM25 will look artificially strong and hybrid will look like it barely helps. The only honest evaluation uses queries written by people who did not see the corpus — or at minimum, paraphrased queries that deliberately avoid document terms.\n\n## A minimal hybrid retriever you can actually run\n\nThe following uses `rank_bm25` and a sentence-transformers model, both real and widely used. It implements RRF over BM25 and cosine similarity, with no external services.\n\n```python\nfrom rank_bm25 import BM25Okapi\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nimport numpy as np\n\ndocs = [\n    \"Reset the credential cache after password rotation.\",\n    \"ERR_CONNECTION_RESET occurs when the TLS handshake fails.\",\n    \"Thermal throttling reduces CPU frequency under sustained load.\",\n]\ntokenized = [d.lower().split() for d in docs]\nbm25 = BM25Okapi(tokenized)\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\ndoc_emb = model.encode(docs, normalize_embeddings=True)\n\ndef rrf(rank_lists, k=60):\n    scores = {}\n    for ranks in rank_lists:\n        for rank, idx in enumerate(ranks):\n            scores[idx] = scores.get(idx, 0.0) + 1.0 / (k + rank + 1)\n    return sorted(scores.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1])\n\ndef hybrid(query, top_k=3):\n    bm_scores = bm25.get_scores(query.lower().split())\n    bm_ranks = np.argsort(-bm_scores).tolist()\n    q_emb = model.encode([query], normalize_embeddings=True)[0]\n    dense_scores = doc_emb @ q_emb\n    dense_ranks = np.argsort(-dense_scores).tolist()\n    fused = rrf([bm_ranks, dense_ranks])\n    return [(docs[i], round(s, 4)) for i, s in fused[:top_k]]\n\nprint(hybrid(\"ERR_CONNECTION_RESET\"))\nprint(hybrid(\"why does my CPU slow down when hot\"))\n```\n\nThe first query is lexical: BM25 ranks the TLS document first, and RRF preserves that. The second is semantic: BM25 finds little overlap, the dense retriever ranks the thermal document first, and RRF again preserves it. This is the mechanism hybrid exploits — not that fusion is smarter than either retriever, but that it lets the stronger retriever for each query type win without a hard-coded switch.\n\n## Who should pick which\n\n**Use BM25 alone** if your corpus is identifier-heavy (logs, code, SKUs, legal citations), your queries are short and keyword-like, and you have no labeled relevance data. Adding dense retrieval adds latency and operational surface for marginal gain, and a badly tuned `alpha` can make results worse. The Elasticsearch default `match` query with a tuned analyzer is a strong baseline that many teams abandon too early.\n\n**Use dense retrieval alone** if your queries are natural-language questions over prose, exact identifiers are rare, and you have the infrastructure to manage an embedding model version alongside your index. The hidden cost is re-embedding the corpus whenever the model changes — a model upgrade is a full reindex, not a config change.\n\n**Use hybrid with RRF** if you have mixed query types and no labeled data. It is the safest default, it needs almost no tuning, and it degrades gracefully when one retriever is weak. Accept that it underweights dominant signals on queries where one retriever clearly wins.\n\n**Use hybrid with weighted fusion or a cross-encoder reranker** if you have relevance judgments and a latency budget that allows a reranking pass. This is where the real gains are, but the gains are only measurable against a clean evaluation set. Without one, you are tuning `alpha` to a feeling.\n\n**Do not use hybrid** if your latency budget is tight and your queries are homogeneous. A cascade (BM25 first, dense only on low-confidence results) often captures most of the benefit at a fraction of the cost.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Does hybrid search always beat BM25?** No. On corpora where queries are keyword-like and documents are short, BM25 alone often matches hybrid, and hybrid adds latency. The reported gains from hybrid come disproportionately from corpora with natural-language queries and vocabulary mismatch. If your evaluation set is drawn from document vocabulary, hybrid will look worse than it is — or BM25 will look better.\n\n**What is the best value for RRF's k?** The commonly cited default is 60, from the original RRF paper. Lower values sharpen the contribution of top-ranked results; higher values flatten the curve. In practice, 60 is a reasonable starting point and the ranking is not highly sensitive to it across a moderate range. Tune it only if you have labeled data; otherwise leave it.\n\n**Can I use BM25 and dense retrieval in the same vector database?** Some vector databases support sparse vectors (which can encode BM25-style term weights) alongside dense vectors, allowing both to be stored and queried in one system. This reduces operational complexity but does not eliminate the fusion decision — you still choose RRF or weighted combination at query time. Check whether the sparse representation matches your analyzer's tokenization before assuming parity with a dedicated BM25 implementation.\n\n**Why does my hybrid system return worse results than either retriever alone?** The most common cause is score normalization: min-max normalizing BM25 scores per query can invert the ranking when the score distribution is skewed. A second cause is a dense model that is out-of-domain, producing near-random similarities that RRF then treats as meaningful. A third is duplicate documents across the two lists being counted twice under naive score addition. RRF avoids the first and third; the second requires checking the embedding model against a sample of queries.\n\n## The next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your retrieval evaluation set — or, if you do not have one, pull 20 real queries from your search logs and write down the document each should return. Run your current BM25-only system against those 20 queries and record which ones fail. Sort the failures into \"vocabulary mismatch\" and \"identifier missed.\" If the failures are mostly vocabulary mismatch, hybrid is worth building; if they are mostly identifier misses, fix your analyzer before adding a second retriever. That one sort tells you more than any benchmark table.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [pgvector README](https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
